<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wilhelm I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1797">1797</a>–<a href="/years/1888">1888</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wilhelm I was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from 1861 and the first emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> from 1871 until his death in 1888. With his chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>, he presided over the wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> under Prussian leadership. He was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> in 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i.jpg" alt="Wilhelm I" width="312" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilhelm Kuntzemüller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaiser_Wilhelm_I_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wilhelm was born on 22 March 1797 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, the second son of Prince Frederick William and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louise-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Louise of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</a>, and was not expected to become king. His father became King Frederick William III the year he was born. Appointed an officer at twelve, he served in the Wars of Liberation against Napoleon I from 1814, won the Iron Cross, and fought at Ligny and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher</a>. He rose to commanding general of the III Army Corps by 1825 and argued for a strong, well-equipped army. In 1829 he married Princess Augusta of Saxe-Weimar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>.</p><p>His brother became King Frederick William IV in 1840, and Wilhelm, as heir, took the title Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848</a> he put down a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, which earned him the nickname Kartätschenprinz, the Prince of Grapeshot, and he briefly fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. After the king's stroke in 1857 he became regent in 1858 and swore an oath on the Prussian constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Wilhelm became king on 2 January 1861 and crowned himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> on 18 October. In 1862 the liberal Landtag refused the budget for an army reform that raised the peacetime army from 150,000 to 200,000 men. Wilhelm first thought of abdicating, then on the advice of his war minister Albrecht von Roon appointed Otto von Bismarck Minister President. Bismarck effectively directed affairs and on several occasions won Wilhelm's assent by threatening to resign.</p><p>Wilhelm commanded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a> of 1864 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866, in which his army defeated the Austrians at Königgratz. He wanted to march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> but was dissuaded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Bismarck</a> and his son, Crown Prince Frederick. In 1867 he became head of the North German Confederation. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> he commanded at Sedan and Gravelotte. He accepted the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> reluctantly, fearing it would overshadow that of King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and was proclaimed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> on 18 January 1871. He left most of the governing to Bismarck.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In May and June 1878 the anarchists Max Hödel and Karl Nobiling made attempts on his life; Nobiling's shots wounded him severely. They gave Bismarck's government the pretext for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws">Anti-Socialist Laws</a> of 19 October 1878, which lapsed in 1890. He later agreed to the Dual Alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, directed against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, again after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Bismarck</a> threatened to resign.</p><p>Wilhelm died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 9 March 1888, aged 90, and was succeeded by Frederick III, who reigned 99 days. Many memorials were raised in his honour, among them the Kyffhäuser monument and the mounted statue at the Deutsches Eck in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>. In his later years he and Augusta were popular as figures of the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wilhelm I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Wilhelm I</b> of Prussia was declared German Emperor, and the Treaty of Frankfurt that May annexed Alsace-Lorraine and imposed a five-billion-franc indemnity that helped fund early industrial expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…years after Schneckenburger's death, Karl Wilhelm, music director of Krefeld, set the poem again and performed it with his men's chorus on 11 June, the silver wedding anniversary of Prince Wilhelm of Prussia, the future Emperor <b>Wilhelm I</b>. This setting became popular after it was sung at Dresden.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein</a></li><li><blockquote>Kaiser <b>Wilhelm I</b> attended celebrations marking the completion of Cologne Cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-15">15 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The confederation was renamed the German Empire, and <b>William I</b> was proclaimed German Emperor at Versailles on 18 January 1871.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-german-confederation">North German Confederation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kronprinzenpalais, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.4&amp;year=1856" aria-label="Kronprinzenpalais on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4851 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wilhelm I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5301 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5301 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.4&amp;year=1856">What was here in 1856? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
